 ## Overview
-Feed-forward and generalizable prediction maps input images directly to a set of 3D Gaussian parameters $\mathcal{G} = \{\mu, \Sigma, \alpha, c\}$ without per-scene gradient descent [Parent]. It solves the latency bottleneck of traditional 3D Gaussian Splatting, which requires minutes of optimization per scene, by learning a prior over scenes that allows inference in milliseconds [Parent]. It differs from sibling methods like [HFGaussian](16422-hfgaussian.md) and [ZeroGS](16425-zerogs.md) by focusing on the architectural design of the encoder-decoder pipeline that generalizes across unseen scenes, whereas siblings may focus on specific constraints or initialization strategies [Parent].
+Feed-forward and generalizable prediction maps input images directly to a 3D Gaussian scene representation $\mathcal{G}$ in a single forward pass, eliminating the per-scene gradient descent optimization required by standard 3D Gaussian Splatting [Parent]. This family solves the latency bottleneck of traditional reconstruction, enabling real-time inference on unseen scenes by learning geometric and appearance priors from large-scale datasets [Parent]. Unlike sibling methods that may rely on specific input constraints (e.g., calibrated poses or sparse views), this family encompasses approaches ranging from uncalibrated stereo pairs to large-scale multi-view sequences, distinguished by their use of learned feature encoders and decoders to predict primitive parameters directly [Parent].
 
